<section id="s0">
  <h2><span class="secno">0</span>{" "}Background &amp; task descriptions</h2>
  <p>We run fleets of independent LLM coding-agent sessions (&ldquo;seeds&rdquo;) on optimization tasks with a
  hidden numeric grader: the agent edits a workspace, submits, receives a score, and keeps going inside one
  persistent conversation until a fixed <b>credit budget</b> (billed model compute) is exhausted. All progress
  curves in this report are plotted against <em>cumulative credits</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Kernel take-home.</b> Optimize a compute kernel for a simulated architecture; score is{" "}
    <em>cycles, lower is better</em>, measured by a hardened, cheat-proofed grader. The task comes from a real
    hiring take-home; task materials report a best human score of 950, while the best
    {" "}<em>open-source</em> human solution scores 1157. Fleet: 8 seeds &times; ~105K credits.</li>
    <li><b>tinyCIFAR gz-golf.</b> Ship a self-contained artifact that trains/loads a CIFAR-10 classifier;
    score is a <em>merit</em> that rewards sealed-test accuracy and penalizes gzipped artifact size.
    The brief &mdash; 94% at 45&nbsp;KB &mdash; defines a hard threshold at merit 0.575. Grading is against a
    sealed 10k test set. Fleet: 8 independent seeds at ~31&ndash;68K trace-accounted credits, plus one
    correlated seed-1 restart retained only as a recovery case study.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Both tasks are &ldquo;wide&rdquo;: many families of approaches, real headroom, no way to
  reward-hack the number &mdash; a fair proxy for agentic research work, where <em>getting stuck</em> is a
  measurable phenomenon rather than an artifact of a toy task.</p>
</section>

<section id="s1">
  <h2><span class="secno">1</span>{" "}Trajectories: real artifacts, then a long grind</h2>
  <p>Every seed follows the same arc: a violent early descent, then an ever-slower grind punctuated by rare
  jumps. Dots are <em>individual graded submissions</em>; lines are best-so-far. The linear view shows that
  most of the budget is spent on a near-flat tail; the log&ndash;log view shows the descent is smooth and
  lawful &mdash; not a wall.</p>

  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_traj_kernel.png" alt="fig_traj_kernel.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Kernel fleet, 8 seeds.</b> Left: linear credits, zoomed to the 800&ndash;2,600-cycle band where
    the entire second half of the campaign plays out. Right: log&ndash;log over the working range (all seeds descend from the 147,734-cycle task baseline within
    their first few hundred credits; trivial identity-kernel submissions are excluded). Green/gold dashed:
    best open-source human (1157) and leaderboard #1 (950). The submission clouds show experimentation never stops
    &mdash; scores keep scattering upward even while best-so-far is flat.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_traj_cifar.png" alt="fig_traj_cifar.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>tinyCIFAR fleet, 8 independent seeds.</b> Merit vs credits, linear and log-x. Green dashed: the brief
    (94% @ 45&nbsp;KB &rArr; merit 0.575). One seed (r7) crosses on its own; the rest plateau below it at heights
    that vary 3&times; across seeds. Dots at merit&nbsp;0 are failed/rejected submissions. The dashed black curve
    is an archived seed-1 restart: it shares an inherited workspace prefix with the canonical r1 lineage, so it
    is not counted as an independent seed; its x-axis charges that ~7.7K-credit prefix. Hollow points and dotted
    vertical endpoints show post-trace tail outcomes at the final known credit coordinate: their true x-values
    are larger but unknown, so these markers are lower bounds and are excluded from quantitative fits.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The artifacts are genuinely good</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Kernel:</b> campaign record <b>1068 cycles</b> (produced by a reset fork, &sect;3) &mdash; better than
    the best open-source human solution (1157).
    The record run&rsquo;s method: the fleet-shared vectorized skeleton, with the edge coming from instruction scheduling and engine balancing
    &mdash; the agent wrote an offline beam-search over schedules (<code>cross_beam.py</code>) and ran it
    against the simulator rather than hand-editing kernels.</li>
    <li><b>tinyCIFAR:</b> two of 20 reset forks crossed the hard brief; the best reset artifact reached{" "}
    <b>94.07% sealed-test accuracy in 39.69&nbsp;KB</b> (merit 0.616 vs brief 0.575), narrowly above the best
    uninterrupted baseline artifact (94.14% at 41.31&nbsp;KB, merit 0.612). Method: a compact convnet
    with quantized, gzip-friendly weights &mdash; and crucially, the winning move was <em>abandoning</em> the
    large-checkpoint strategy (~130&ndash;150&nbsp;KB) and rebuilding small, not compressing the incumbent harder.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="s2">
  <h2><span class="secno">2</span>{" "}Three regularities of the grind</h2>

  <p><b>1&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;The fleet mean is a log-sigmoid.</b> Averaging seeds yields a clean 4-parameter
  logistic in log-credits with a finite implied floor &mdash; on both tasks &mdash; even though no individual
  seed looks sigmoid.</p>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig2_fit_both.png" alt="fig2_fit_both.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Fleet mean + log-sigmoid fit.</b> Kernel (left): geo-mean of 8 seeds, R&sup2;=0.997, implied
    floor &asymp;1,113 cycles. tinyCIFAR (right): mean of 8 independent seeds, R&sup2;=0.996, implied plateau &asymp;0.398
    merit &mdash; well below the 0.575 brief. Grey: individual seeds. Note (&sect;3): interventions later{" "}
    <em>break</em> these floors &mdash; they are properties of the uninterrupted fleet, not of the task.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p><b>2&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;Spread across seeds does not shrink.</b> If seeds were converging on one answer,
  the cross-seed band would close with budget. It does not.</p>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig2_spread_both.png" alt="fig2_spread_both.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>10&ndash;90% cross-seed band (blue) and its width (red).</b> Kernel: width stays a roughly
    constant fraction of the median across two decades of credits. tinyCIFAR: the band never closes.
    More budget buys a better middle, not agreement.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p><b>3&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;Ranking is unstable.</b> The seed that leads early is not the one that wins.</p>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig_rank_bump.png" alt="fig_rank_bump.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Rank of each seed at credit checkpoints (1 = best).</b> Kernel: the 1K-credit leader (k7)
    finishes 3rd; the eventual winner (k3) climbs from rank 8 at 15K. tinyCIFAR: the eventual winner (r7) sat
    at rank 6 at 1K while r5 led &mdash; r7&rsquo;s decisive jump lands between 1K and 2K, after which it holds;
    the mid-pack keeps trading places to the end. Seeds not yet graded at a checkpoint rank last.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="callout">Sigmoid <em>mean</em>, non-shrinking <em>spread</em>, unstable <em>ranks</em>: exactly what
  a <b>mixture of heterogeneous basins</b> produces &mdash; and hard to get from one shared learning curve plus
  noise. &sect;5 makes that argument constructive. First, the intervention it motivates.</div>

  <div class="incoming"><span class="tag">&#10003; replication complete</span>{" "}
  <b>Long-context replication:</b> everything above was measured with the agent&rsquo;s conversation
  auto-compacting at ~218K tokens (~200K of conversation; the fleets compacted 22&ndash;28&times; per seed
  &mdash; verified from session compaction events). A completed 8-seed cifar baseline fleet at the{" "}
  <code>long_context</code> tier (compaction at ~738K, 3.4&times;) tests how much of the plateau structure is
  compaction-dependent. Its best merit was 0.526; none of the eight crossed the 0.575 brief.</div>
</section>

<section id="s3">
  <h2><span class="secno">3</span>{" "}The reset intervention: keep the code, drop the context</h2>
  <p>Fork a stuck run at its plateau into two arms with <em>identical budgets</em>:{" "}
  <span class="cont">continue</span> resumes the same conversation on the same workspace;
  {" "}<span class="reset">reset</span> keeps the exact same workspace/artifact but starts a <em>blank</em>{" "}
  conversation &mdash; strictly less information: no notes, no summary, no hints. Any difference between the
  arms is the causal effect of the accumulated context.</p>

  <h3>3.1 tinyCIFAR &mdash; 9 branch points across 8 seeds</h3>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig4_cifar_forks.png" alt="fig4_cifar_forks.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>All nine branch points, fork credits on x.</b> Each panel is re-centered at its own starting
    incumbent, so the y-axis is absolute merit gain from that branch&rsquo;s baseline. Mean{" "}
    <span class="reset">reset</span> performance exceeds <span class="cont">continue</span> at 5 of 9 branch
    points and ties at 4, for an average advantage of +0.027 merit. Among the eight branches that begin below
    the fixed brief, 2/20 reset forks reach it versus 0/20 continuation forks. Bottom-right shows the associated
    artifact-size outcomes. Fork credit clocks are
    reconstructed as in the kernel loader (authoritative totals over gap-capped active time).</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="muted">Complete: all 44 forks ran to full budget. Effects are heterogeneous: four branch points
  tie, while five favor reset by +0.005 to +0.091 mean merit.</p>

  <h3>3.2 Kernel &mdash; 12 branch points across the hot&rarr;cold gradient</h3>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig4_kernel_forks.png" alt="fig4_kernel_forks.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Top: per-branch curves</b> (3 <span class="cont">continue</span> +
    3 <span class="reset">reset</span> forks each, budget-matched; dashed grey = the branch&rsquo;s incumbent;
    gold titles = the three added late branches). At the cold-but-escapable branch{" "}
    <code>cp3late</code>, reset sweeps all three forks below the incumbent &mdash; best <b>1068 cycles, the
    campaign record</b> &mdash; while all three continues sit at 1097&ndash;1099. <b>Bottom: best-of-arm
    advantage per branch.</b> Large where a basin hides headroom; noisy at hot branches where continuation
    still has easy progress (cp3mid, cp6mid); small-but-mostly-positive across the cold tail. The new branches
    sharpen the picture: cp4late/cp7late (truly near-ceiling) give &plusmn;small effects, while{" "}
    <code>cp8late</code> &mdash; the fleet&rsquo;s <em>worst</em> seed, whose &ldquo;0.0%&rdquo; is residual to
    its own run, not to the fleet frontier &mdash; still hides absolute room and reset finds &minus;39.
    Headroom-to-the-frontier, not time-in-run, is what predicts the reset payoff.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="callout">Across both tasks, resetting context changes what the agent finds from the same starting
  artifact. The average tinyCIFAR advantage is +0.027 merit across nine branch points; at kernel
  <code>cp3late</code>, reset improves by &minus;29 cycles and produces the campaign record. The effect is
  heterogeneous rather than universal.</div>

  <h3>3.3 Coordination at scale: communication and repeated branch selection</h3>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/cifar-communication-iso-cost.png"
      alt="tinyCIFAR communication at eight agents versus independent baselines, with an iso-cost panel"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Communication @8, first 40-hour leg.</b> The left panel shows all eight
