  </figure>
</section>

<section id="s4">
  <h2><span class="secno">4</span>{" "}Advanced interventions built on reset</h2>
  <p>Plain reset wastes nothing but the context &mdash; but it also learns nothing from the discarded branch.
  Three protocols, smoke-tested on the kernel task, push past that:</p>
  <div class="protos">
    <div class="proto">
      <h4>Branch-select <span class="status">smoke passed</span></h4>
      <div class="flow">main leg runs &#8595;
stall detected (no gain for S credits)
&#8594; fork K reset branches
&#8594; race them on equal budget
&#8594; best branch becomes main; rest dropped</div>
      <p>Automates &sect;3 as a <em>policy</em>: a stall detector triggers the fork; selection keeps fleet width
      at 1. Controls: K=0 (plain continue) and K=1 (reset without selection).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="proto">
      <h4>Branch-distill <span class="status">smoke passed</span></h4>
      <div class="flow">fork K reset branches &#8595;
each writes a post-mortem note
&#8594; distiller merges: winner workspace
   + ranked all-branch report
&#8594; ONE distill leg resumes with the merged material</div>
      <p>Selection discards K&minus;1 branches&rsquo; discoveries; distillation folds them back in as a
      structured note on the winner&rsquo;s workspace.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="proto">
      <h4>Board (broadcast) <span class="status">smoke passed</span></h4>
      <div class="flow">N concurrent runs &#8595;
each episode: post score + 1-line STATUS
to a shared board; read peers&rsquo; entries
control: same N, no board</div>
      <p>The lightest-touch social intervention: does merely seeing that a peer is ahead (and roughly how)
      decorrelate basins the way a reset does?</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="incoming"><span class="tag">&#9679; data incoming</span>{" "}
  All three protocols have validated drivers and passed smoke runs on the kernel task; <b>powered branch-select
  and board arms are active</b>, while branch-distill remains queued. Per-protocol results (vs K=0 / K=1
  controls) will slot in here.</div>
</section>

<section id="s5">
  <h2><span class="secno">5</span>{" "}Why resets can work: a simple model of performance curves</h2>
  <p>Let <i>Q</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>t</i>) be seed <i>i</i>&rsquo;s best performance by budget <i>t</i>. Each
  realized curve is a staircase: improvements arrive at irregular times, while the fleet mean can look smooth.
  The reset argument does not begin by assuming a particular basin mechanism. It begins with the distribution of
  those waiting times.</p>

  <div class="theory-grid">
    <div class="theory-card">
      <span class="theory-label">Performance curve</span>
      <div class="theory-equation"><i>Q</i>(<i>t</i>) = best quality by budget <i>t</i></div>
      <p>Fixing a budget gives the cross-seed distribution of performance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="theory-card">
      <span class="theory-label">Qualified runtime</span>
      <div class="theory-equation"><i>T</i><sub>q</sub> = inf{"{"}<i>t</i> : <i>Q</i>(<i>t</i>) &ge; <i>q</i>{"}"}</div>
      <p>Fixing a quality level gives the distribution of time required to reach it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="theory-card theory-card--decision">
      <span class="theory-label">Reset decision</span>
      <div class="theory-equation"><i>c</i> + E[<i>W</i><sub>reset</sub>] &lt; <i>m</i>(<i>s</i>)</div>
      <p>Reset when a redraw costs less than the expected remaining wait under continuation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Performance curves and runtime distributions are two views of the same object</h3>
  <p>For any monotone best-so-far curve and target quality <i>q</i>,</p>
  <div class="theory-equation theory-equation--standalone">
    P(<i>Q</i>(<i>t</i>) &ge; <i>q</i>) = P(<i>T</i><sub>q</sub> &le; <i>t</i>).
  </div>
  <p>The left side is a horizontal slice through the fleet at matched budget; the right side is the classical
  runtime distribution, generalized from &ldquo;time to solve&rdquo; to &ldquo;time to quality <i>q</i>.&rdquo;
  Inter-improvement waits are the local version of the same construction. This identity is the bridge from
  randomized-search restart theory to anytime auto-research curves.</p>

  <h3>The waiting-time distribution determines the restart decision</h3>
  <p>Let <i>W</i> be the wait to the next improvement, and suppose the run has already stagnated for
  <i>s</i>. Its <b>mean residual life</b> is</p>
  <div class="theory-equation theory-equation--standalone">
    <i>m</i>(<i>s</i>) = E[<i>W</i> &minus; <i>s</i> &mid; <i>W</i> &gt; <i>s</i>].
  </div>
  <p>Continuing inherits that conditional wait. A reset incurs cost <i>c</i> and draws a new wait
  <i>W</i><sub>reset</sub>. Therefore the local decision is simply</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>Reset condition.</b>{" "}
    Reset when <i>c</i> + E[<i>W</i><sub>reset</sub>] &lt; <i>m</i>(<i>s</i>); otherwise continue.
  </div>
  <p>For the classical i.i.d. restart, <i>W</i><sub>reset</sub> has the same distribution as <i>W</i>. A
  deterministic cutoff <i>s</i> then has expected completion time</p>
  <div class="theory-equation theory-equation--standalone">
    <i>R</i>(<i>s</i>) =
    (&int;<sub>0</sub><sup><i>s</i></sup> <i>S</i>(<i>u</i>) d<i>u</i> +
    <i>cS</i>(<i>s</i>)) / (1 &minus; <i>S</i>(<i>s</i>)),
  </div>
  <p>where <i>S</i>(<i>s</i>) = P(<i>W</i> &gt; <i>s</i>). This beats uninterrupted search exactly when
  <i>m</i>(<i>s</i>) &gt; E[<i>W</i>] + <i>c</i>. Thus the full waiting-time distribution dictates whether a
  useful cutoff exists. Decreasing hazard is a sufficient condition for rising mean residual life. Under formal
  long-tailedness, the residual wait eventually exceeds any fixed redraw cost, so an independent cutoff restart
  becomes advantageous. Empirically, the safer test is the residual-wait curve itself: no Pareto fit is required.</p>

  <h3>A rate mixture explains why a long wait can be informative</h3>
  <p>The restart result above needs no latent-basin story. A mixture supplies one mechanism. At a fixed quality,
  suppose a search state <i>b</i> has an approximately memoryless improvement wait
  <i>W</i> &mid; <i>b</i> &sim; Exp(&lambda;<sub>b</sub>). Pooling states gives</p>
  <div class="theory-equation theory-equation--standalone">
    <i>S</i>(<i>w</i>) = &sum;<sub>b</sub> &pi;<sub>b</sub>
    exp(&minus;&lambda;<sub>b</sub><i>w</i>).
  </div>
  <p>After observing no improvement for a long time, posterior weight shifts toward smaller
  &lambda;<sub>b</sub>. The state itself need not deteriorate; stagnation reveals that the current draw was
  probably slow. A reset can help by redrawing that search state.</p>

  <p>We test this explanation with two model organisms. The calibrated trap-mixture implements the mechanism
  directly. The <b>stateful SLS organism</b> runs clause-weighting local search (Breakout/DLM-style) on
  over-constrained MAX-3SAT; accumulated clause weights act as history-derived search state, distinct from the
  current assignment and best assignment.</p>
  <figure>
    <div class="imgbox"><img src="/writing/fig5_organisms.png" alt="fig5_organisms.png"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Rows: trap-mixture simulation (top), stateful-SLS organism (bottom). Columns:</b> (1) the
    fleet mean is a log-sigmoid (R&sup2;=0.996 / 0.981) even though individual runs are step-like and
    heterogeneous &mdash; the sigmoid is an <em>ensemble</em> artifact of averaging over basins; (2) cross-run
    spread does not shrink; (3) the diagnostic waiting-time signature: a single state has approximately flat
    residual wait, while <b>pooled expected-further-wait rises with stagnation</b>. The last column is the
    empirical premise used by the reset inequality above.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>What the classical argument does &mdash; and does not &mdash; establish</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Exact transfer:</b> classical restart theory covers independent cold restarts toward a fixed target.
    The qualified-runtime identity above transfers that logic exactly to fresh attempts on an anytime curve.</li>
    <li><b>Context reset is a warm restart:</b> our experiment holds the workspace fixed and redraws only the
    conversation state. Its post-reset wait need not be i.i.d. with a cold start, which is why the general
    condition uses E[<i>W</i><sub>reset</sub>] rather than assuming E[<i>W</i>]. Preserving the workspace is an
    experimental control here, not a claim that every useful reset must preserve it.</li>
    <li><b>Mechanism versus decision:</b> the waiting-time distribution licenses the decision; the rate-mixture
    model explains one way that distribution can arise. The matched forks in &sect;3 test whether removing
    context actually produces the required redraw from the same starting workspace.</li>
    <li><b>Policy remains empirical:</b> Luby schedules are distribution-agnostic cold-restart schedules.
    Stagnation-triggered context-reset policies must estimate costs and residual waits in this warm setting,
    which is what branch-select in &sect;4 begins to operationalize.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

